The recursive function looks at positions `sourceIndex` and `targetIndex`. When the characters are equal, there are two choices: use the source character or leave it unused. When they differ, only the skip choice is possible.

Results are saved in `memo`, since the same pair of positions can be reached through different choices. For `s = "rabbbit"` and `t = "rabbit"`, the repeated `b` characters create several branches, and the memo table combines their counts instead of solving each branch again.

The base cases are important. Reaching the end of `t` gives one valid way, while reaching the end of `s` first gives zero.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    long long countWays(const string& s, const string& t,
                        int sourceIndex, int targetIndex,
                        vector<vector<long long>>& memo) {
        if (targetIndex == static_cast<int>(t.size())) {
            return 1;
        }
        if (sourceIndex == static_cast<int>(s.size())) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (memo[sourceIndex][targetIndex] != -1) {
            return memo[sourceIndex][targetIndex];
        }

        long long ways = countWays(s, t, sourceIndex + 1, targetIndex, memo);
        if (s[sourceIndex] == t[targetIndex]) {
            ways += countWays(s, t, sourceIndex + 1, targetIndex + 1, memo);
        }

        memo[sourceIndex][targetIndex] = ways;
        return ways;
    }

    int distinctSubsequences(string s, string t) {
        if (t.empty()) {
            return 1;
        }
        if (s.size() < t.size()) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<vector<long long>> memo(
            s.size(), vector<long long>(t.size(), -1)
        );
        return static_cast<int>(countWays(s, t, 0, 0, memo));
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(len(s) * len(t))`, because each pair of positions is computed once.
Space complexity: `O(len(s) * len(t))` for the memo table, plus recursion stack space.

This version uses a full table because it makes the states easy to read. `dp[i][j]` stores the number of ways to form the first `j` characters of `t` using the first `i` characters of `s`.

The first column is filled with `1`. Any prefix of `s` can form an empty target by selecting nothing. If the current characters match, the table adds the cases that use the source character and the cases that skip it. Otherwise, it just copies the value from the previous source prefix.

```java
class Solution {
    public int distinctSubsequences(String s, String t) {
        int sourceLength = s.length();
        int targetLength = t.length();
        long[][] dp = new long[sourceLength + 1][targetLength + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i <= sourceLength; i++) {
            dp[i][0] = 1;
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= sourceLength; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= targetLength; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j];

                if (s.charAt(i - 1) == t.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] += dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                }
            }
        }

        return (int) dp[sourceLength][targetLength];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(len(s) * len(t))`.
Space complexity: `O(len(s) * len(t))`. The `long` table gives safer intermediate additions before the required integer result is returned.

Here the state is based on the remaining suffixes. `dp[i][j]` tells how many ways the suffix `t[j:]` can be formed from `s[i:]`.

Every state can skip `s[i]`. If the characters match, it can also use `s[i]` and move forward in both strings. Filling from the end means both needed states are already known.

The edge cases are built into the table: an empty remaining target has one way, while a non-empty target has zero ways when the source is empty.

```go
func DistinctSubsequences(s string, t string) int {
    sourceLength := len(s)
    targetLength := len(t)

    dp := make([][]int64, sourceLength+1)
    for i := 0; i <= sourceLength; i++ {
        dp[i] = make([]int64, targetLength+1)
        dp[i][targetLength] = 1
    }

    for i := sourceLength - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
        for j := targetLength - 1; j >= 0; j-- {
            dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j]
            if s[i] == t[j] {
                dp[i][j] += dp[i+1][j+1]
            }
        }
    }

    return int(dp[0][0])
}
```

Time complexity: `O(len(s) * len(t))`.
Space complexity: `O(len(s) * len(t))`.

This follows the usual take-or-skip idea, but stores the answer for every pair of remaining lengths. `ways(sourceLength, targetLength)` counts how many ways the first `targetLength` characters of `t` can be formed from the first `sourceLength` characters of `s`.

If the last characters match, one group of answers skips `s[sourceLength - 1]`, and another group uses it. If they do not match, the last source character must be skipped.

A quick example is `s = "aaa"`, `t = "aa"`. The result is `3`, because any two of the three `a` characters can be selected. The empty target returns `1`, and a target longer than the source returns `0`.

```java
class Solution {
    private int solve(String s, String t, int sourceLength,
                      int targetLength, long[][] memo) {
        if (targetLength == 0) {
            return 1;
        }
        if (sourceLength == 0 || targetLength > sourceLength) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (memo[sourceLength][targetLength] != -1) {
            return (int) memo[sourceLength][targetLength];
        }

        long ways = solve(s, t, sourceLength - 1, targetLength, memo);
        if (s.charAt(sourceLength - 1) == t.charAt(targetLength - 1)) {
            ways += solve(s, t, sourceLength - 1, targetLength - 1, memo);
        }

        memo[sourceLength][targetLength] = ways;
        return (int) ways;
    }

    public int distinctSubsequences(String s, String t) {
        int sourceLength = s.length();
        int targetLength = t.length();
        long[][] memo = new long[sourceLength + 1][targetLength + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i <= sourceLength; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j <= targetLength; j++) {
                memo[i][j] = -1;
            }
        }

        return solve(s, t, sourceLength, targetLength, memo);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(len(s) * len(t))` after memoization.
Space complexity: `O(len(s) * len(t))` for the table, with up to `O(len(s) + len(t))` recursive stack space.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{
  public: int distinctSubsequences(string s, string t){
	   int n = s.size();
        int m = t.size();
        vector<vector<long long >>dp(n + 1,vector<long long>(m + 1, 0));

        for(int i=0;i<=n;i++){ // for empty string in t
          dp[i][0]=1; 
        }
        for(int i=1;i<=n;i++){
            for(int j=1;j<=m;j++){
                if(s[i-1]==t[j-1]){ // if char match we have both option of take and not_take
                  dp[i][j]=dp[i-1][j-1]+dp[i-1][j];
                }
                else{
                    dp[i][j]=dp[i-1][j]; // donot match so only skip option is present
                }
            }
        }
       return dp[n][m];
	}
};
```
